WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:04.722
Picture the end of a baking day.

2
00:00:04.722 --> 00:00:11.169
Two trays of cookies come out of the oven, five cookies in all: two oat, two chocolate and one ginger.

3
00:00:11.169 --> 00:00:12.180
Most are golden.

4
00:00:12.180 --> 00:00:20.839
One chocolate cookie is still raw in the middle, the ginger one is burnt, and someone has already taken a 10 gram bite out of an oat one.

5
00:00:21.067 --> 00:00:25.756
There's a cookie jar on the kitchen scale, and it already holds 200 grams.

6
00:00:25.756 --> 00:00:29.939
At the end of the day, each golden cookie goes in, minus any bite.

7
00:00:29.939 --> 00:00:31.967
The raw and burnt ones stay out.

8
00:00:32.767 --> 00:00:35.698
Here's a question that's easy to be shy about.

9
00:00:35.698 --> 00:00:37.355
map and filter make sense.

10
00:00:37.355 --> 00:00:41.498
But whenever I need one total, I'm back to a for loop with a var.

11
00:00:41.498 --> 00:00:42.390
Is that wrong?

12
00:00:42.390 --> 00:00:45.131
And what is this fold everyone talks about?

13
00:00:46.033 --> 00:00:48.826
Let's start with the loop we'd all write.

14
00:00:48.826 --> 00:00:50.052
jar starts at 200.

15
00:00:50.052 --> 00:00:54.342
For each cookie, if it's golden, add its grams, minus the bite.

16
00:00:54.342 --> 00:00:56.385
At the end, jar is the answer.

17
00:00:56.600 --> 00:00:57.816
Look at its shape.

18
00:00:57.816 --> 00:01:01.058
Every loop that builds one result has two parts.

19
00:01:01.058 --> 00:01:02.882
A starting value, here 200.

20
00:01:02.882 --> 00:01:08.893
And a step, which takes the result so far and the next cookie, and gives the next result.

21
00:01:09.800 --> 00:01:12.612
Let's walk it cookie by cookie, with the scale.

22
00:01:12.612 --> 00:01:15.125
Before we do: where will the scale end up?

23
00:01:18.100 --> 00:01:19.634
Start at 200.

24
00:01:19.634 --> 00:01:24.473
c1 is a golden oat cookie, 45 grams: 245.

25
00:01:24.473 --> 00:01:28.957
c2 is raw, so it stays out: still 245.

26
00:01:29.167 --> 00:01:36.507
c3 is golden, 50 grams, but with a 10 gram bite, so 40 more: 285.

27
00:01:36.507 --> 00:01:39.104
c4 is burnt: still 285.

28
00:01:39.104 --> 00:01:43.395
c5 is golden chocolate, 30 grams: 315.

29
00:01:45.200 --> 00:01:46.287
Now let's map it.

30
00:01:46.287 --> 00:01:51.020
The reading on the scale is the one value you carry from cookie to cookie.

31
00:01:51.020 --> 00:01:54.154
The 200 already in the jar is the starting value.

32
00:01:54.154 --> 00:01:57.288
Your rule at each cookie, in or out, is the step.

33
00:01:57.288 --> 00:01:59.527
And the last reading is the answer.

34
00:02:00.433 --> 00:02:01.343
Now the names.

35
00:02:01.343 --> 00:02:04.720
The value you carry along is called the accumulator.

36
00:02:04.720 --> 00:02:14.917
And fold is that loop, with both parts passed in: the starting value in parentheses, and the step as a lambda that gets the accumulator and the next element.

37
00:02:15.833 --> 00:02:16.888
Here's the jar.

38
00:02:16.888 --> 00:02:20.121
cookies.fold, starting at 200, with jar and c.

39
00:02:20.121 --> 00:02:24.127
A golden cookie gives jar plus its grams, minus the bite.

40
00:02:24.127 --> 00:02:26.516
Raw or burnt gives jar, unchanged.

41
00:02:26.516 --> 00:02:34.107
Java's stream reduce takes the same two parts, plus a third argument that only matters for parallel streams.

42
00:02:35.033 --> 00:02:36.595
So where did the var go?

43
00:02:36.595 --> 00:02:44.271
The standard library's fold is the loop you'd write by hand: a var set to the start, a for loop, one step per element.

44
00:02:44.271 --> 00:02:48.369
And it's inline, so your lambda is pasted right into that loop.

45
00:02:48.369 --> 00:02:49.995
The var didn't disappear.

46
00:02:49.995 --> 00:02:55.589
It moved inside fold, where nobody else can touch it, and your own code has only vals.

47
00:02:56.500 --> 00:02:58.616
You've been using folds already.

48
00:02:58.616 --> 00:03:04.040
sumOf, count and maxByOrNull are special cases of fold, already written and named.

49
00:03:04.040 --> 00:03:08.339
The sum of all five weights is 295, and three cookies are golden.

50
00:03:08.339 --> 00:03:12.638
Prefer them when they fit: sumOf says more than a fold that adds.

51
00:03:12.638 --> 00:03:16.672
Reach for fold when the step needs a when, like the jar does.

52
00:03:17.600 --> 00:03:19.227
And then there's reduce.

53
00:03:19.227 --> 00:03:25.598
It looks like fold without the starting value: it uses the first element as the start instead.

54
00:03:25.598 --> 00:03:29.936
It's shorter, and on a normal day it gives the same answer, 295.

55
00:03:29.936 --> 00:03:32.240
So why ever type a starting value?

56
00:03:33.167 --> 00:03:37.210
Here's a day when nothing was baked: an empty list of weights.

57
00:03:37.210 --> 00:03:39.558
fold starting at 0, sum, and reduce.

58
00:03:39.558 --> 00:03:41.123
What does each one give?

59
00:03:44.100 --> 00:03:45.135
fold gives 0.

60
00:03:45.135 --> 00:03:46.090
sum gives 0.

61
00:03:46.090 --> 00:03:50.308
And reduce throws: Empty collection can't be reduced.

62
00:03:50.308 --> 00:03:53.492
first fails the same way: List is empty.

63
00:03:54.200 --> 00:03:55.299
Think of the jar.

64
00:03:55.299 --> 00:04:01.180
On an empty day, fold still has a jar on the scale, and the scale shows the starting value.

65
00:04:01.180 --> 00:04:06.092
reduce has nothing to put on the scale at all, so all it can do is complain.

66
00:04:06.092 --> 00:04:09.065
A quiet day in the kitchen isn't an emergency.

67
00:04:09.065 --> 00:04:10.745
reduce treats it like one.

68
00:04:11.667 --> 00:04:13.494
Kotlin gives you two names.

69
00:04:13.494 --> 00:04:16.404
reduce throws, and reduceOrNull gives null.

70
00:04:16.404 --> 00:04:19.178
first throws, and firstOrNull gives null.

71
00:04:19.178 --> 00:04:20.735
Java chose differently.

72
00:04:20.735 --> 00:04:29.193
Its reduce without a starting value returns an Optional, a box that may be empty, so the type makes you handle the empty day.

73
00:04:30.100 --> 00:04:32.033
The jar picture has one limit.

74
00:04:32.033 --> 00:04:38.026
A scale shows one number, but an accumulator can be anything: a list, a map, even a function.

75
00:04:38.026 --> 00:04:45.565
The last lesson folded a list of recipe adjustments into one function, starting from a function that changes nothing.

76
00:04:46.467 --> 00:04:47.776
So here's the rule.

77
00:04:47.776 --> 00:04:49.844
An empty list is a normal day.

78
00:04:49.844 --> 00:04:54.739
Use fold, sumOf, or an OrNull function whenever the input can be empty.

79
00:04:54.739 --> 00:05:01.149
Keep reduce and first for places where an empty list is a bug you want to hear about, loudly.

80
00:05:02.067 --> 00:05:04.616
So, is the for loop with a var wrong?

81
00:05:04.616 --> 00:05:04.823
No.

82
00:05:04.823 --> 00:05:07.097
It's a fold, written out by hand.

83
00:05:07.097 --> 00:05:13.229
fold is that loop, with the start and the step passed in, and the var tucked away inside.

84
00:05:13.433 --> 00:05:14.300
Quick check.

85
00:05:14.300 --> 00:05:16.394
A day with no cookies at all.

86
00:05:16.394 --> 00:05:19.932
cookies.fold, starting at 200, with the jar rule.

87
00:05:19.932 --> 00:05:21.737
What does the scale show?

88
00:05:24.733 --> 00:05:25.023
200.

89
00:05:25.023 --> 00:05:29.084
With nothing to add, fold hands back the starting value.

90
00:05:29.084 --> 00:05:31.623
That's exactly why it never throws.
